# Expert Hub API — SECRETS env file. ⚠️ NEVER COMMIT A COPY WITH VALUES.
#
# Copy to `api.secrets.env` on the server (that name is gitignored) and pass it
# as a SECOND --env-file so compose can hand the values to the API container
# only — the frontend container never sees them (its entrypoint refuses to
# start if EXPERT_HUB_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET reaches it, P-159):
#
#   docker compose --env-file env/local.env --env-file env/api.secrets.env up -d --build
#
# Both values are OPTIONAL today:
#  - no client secret  ⇒ public client, PKCE-only (works — P-163)
#  - no connection string ⇒ the API boots; /health/ready reports the missing
#    database honestly; the SSO handshake does not need the database.

# SQL Server. For the compose-stack database (--profile db): pick a STRONG sa
# password (SQL Server enforces complexity: 8+ chars, upper/lower/digit) and
# use the matching connection string:
#   EXPERT_HUB_DB_SA_PASSWORD=<strong password>
#   EXPERT_HUB_API_CONNECTION_STRING=Server=expert-hub-db;Database=ExpertHub;User Id=sa;Password=<same password>;TrustServerCertificate=true;Encrypt=false
# For a managed instance later, set only the connection string.
EXPERT_HUB_DB_SA_PASSWORD=
EXPERT_HUB_API_CONNECTION_STRING=

# ── Per-SERVER overrides (not secrets, but not the repository's business) ───
# This file is untracked, and compose applies `--env-file` in order with the
# last one winning — so anything set here overrides `local.env` and survives
# every `git pull`. Put values that are true of THIS machine here rather than
# editing the tracked env file, which causes merge conflicts on deploy.
#
# The first administrator(s) — user id or email, comma-separated (`P-181`):
EXPERT_HUB_BOOTSTRAP_ADMINS=

# OIDC client secret (only if FAST issues one — confidential client):
EXPERT_HUB_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ── Teams — interview meetings (J-06/F2, `P-247`) ──────────────────────────
#
# ⚠️ Empty ClientSecret = the provider is OFF. Nothing is booked, meeting_url
# stays null, and the interview panel behaves exactly as it always has.
#
# ⚠️ Before this works, an Exchange administrator MUST scope the app to the
# organiser mailbox alone. `Calendars.ReadWrite` (Application) otherwise
# reaches EVERY mailbox in the tenant:
#
#   New-ApplicationAccessPolicy -AppId <client-id> `
#     -PolicyScopeGroupId service-teams@fa.gov.sa `
#     -AccessRight RestrictAccess `
#     -Description "Expert Hub - interview invitations only"
#
EXPERT_HUB_TEAMS_TENANT_ID=
EXPERT_HUB_TEAMS_CLIENT_ID=
EXPERT_HUB_TEAMS_CLIENT_SECRET=
EXPERT_HUB_TEAMS_ORGANIZER_UPN=
EXPERT_HUB_TEAMS_INTERVIEW_MINUTES=30

# ── FAST service credential — reference data (INT-05) ──────────────────────
#
# ⚠️ WAITING_FOR_FAST_SERVICE_CREDENTIAL. FAST has not yet issued a client
# for machine-to-machine access (`docs/specification/22_FAST_INTEGRATION_REQUEST.md`),
# and nothing reads these two until the service token provider is built. Leave
# them empty. NEVER put a person's access token here.
EXPERT_HUB_FAST_CLIENT_ID=
EXPERT_HUB_FAST_CLIENT_SECRET=

# INT-06 — the AI provider's API key (Anthropic today; OpenAI later).
EXPERT_HUB_AI_API_KEY=
